ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186286456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287570 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186286962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287570 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287570 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287570 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186284963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287570 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cc5ac333-7088-55b5-8dff-47ad3124a244" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287751 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287751 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181775" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287751 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287751 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=R\\times R$ in the Cartesian plane?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287751 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1e98098c-88d7-594b-ad3c-687cf15456dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287751 #edb/ref 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set\n\n$$\nS=R\\times R\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in R,\\,y\\in R\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{0}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $0$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{0}=\\{(0,y)\\,:\\,y\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{0}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/07/07a3d9a6c38b5bfcc529496735a1a66eaceefd5ae3437a2cb0cd45911eccafae.png)\n\nNow, consider a general ${S}_{i}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals some $i\\in R$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{i}=\\{(i,y)\\,:\\,y\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nFor each $i\\in R,$ ${S}_{i}$ is a vertical line.\n\n![](images/e4/e454f1cf125486ca25acf15e26efd251a4a03b08e899814ab3e77db7898713c4.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product $S$ is the union of these subsets over $R:$\n\n$$\nS={⋃}_{i\\in R}{S}_{i}.\n$$\n\nThis is the whole plane, as shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/07/070389fcdb6d69f9aa90dabbd98a0d3461e26d3e3e08b1d53b92e99688c09923.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287777 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287513 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287777 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181966" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287777 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287777 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=Z\\times Z$ in the Cartesian plane?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287777 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8fc924c0-3fd1-5974-a50f-be993c31ee9b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287777 #edb/ref 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=Z\\times Z\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is an integer number and whose $y$-coordinate is an integer number.\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in Z,\\,y\\in Z\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any integer number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}=\\{(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in Z\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/96/9673951b4e72ba503e740369462f9e528e43ffc083706ee558ade7ccd52702f5.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{0}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $0$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any integer number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{0}=\\{(0,y)\\,:\\,y\\in Z\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{0}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/79/797b66a293eb241f841869abe97e324f4301907f71029acceefd68487bef1b4f.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…,{S}_{-3},{S}_{-2},{S}_{1},{S}_{2},{S}_{3},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =…\\cup {S}_{-3}\\cup {S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1}\\cup {S}_{0}\\cup {S}_{1}\\cup \\text{ }{S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}… \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in Z}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $Z\\times Z$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/de/de19fcf0f246b09d4bb957568e026b36de3f55c1a6c7065d79faacee794a8326.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287784 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287784 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181979" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287784 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287784 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S={Z}^{-}\\times {Z}^{-}$ in the Cartesian plane?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287784 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "93d5eecd-9d38-5be6-abbe-17d9f9d1df54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287784 #edb/ref 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS={Z}^{-}\\times {Z}^{-}\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a negative integer and whose $y$-coordinate is a negative integer.\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in {Z}^{-},\\,y\\in {Z}^{-}\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is negative integer.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}=\\{(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in {Z}^{-}\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/37/37ad5627c3fb7cc89e751bb697e01ac7fecaf2712e230b6e3812ad9137d5cd46.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{-2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any negative integer.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-2}=\\{(-2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in {Z}^{-}\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-2}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/97/97ebcac85477b8e88564ab67d59c926d5faa72138c8a31d80d3b89895f33668c.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…{S}_{-5},{S}_{-4},{S}_{-3}$ similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =…{S}_{-5}\\cup {S}_{-4}\\cup {S}_{-3}\\cup {S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1} \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in {Z}^{-}}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize ${Z}^{-}\\times {Z}^{-}$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/a7/a756e53554cb9184d96a52b83038fbd6b00b21d0a12cc658aebb5010e917fd03.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287870 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287870 #edb/ref 1069 "q-184488" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287870 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287870 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=\\{-2,2\\}\\times \\{-1,0,1\\}$ in the Cartesian plane?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287870 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "08598431-bbb6-5074-989f-295156a85943" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287870 #edb/ref 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, computing $\\{-2,2\\}\\times \\{-1,0,1\\}$ gives the following set:\n\n$$\nS=\\{(-2,-1),(-2,0),(-2,1),(2,-1),(2,0),(2,1)\\}.\n$$\n\nOur visualization of this set is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/5d/5dd3ff71d17188adaa68632b6d16aed1f41e1975ec4ddd321a8160fabb0e16b8.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287873 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287873 #edb/ref 1069 "q-184504" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287873 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287873 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=[-i,i]\\times \\{1-i\\}.$ Which of the following represents ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287873 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f55227ad-192f-5284-8e39-2f59b086abb6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287873 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[-0.5,0.5]\\times \\{0.5\\}=\\{(k,0.5)\\,:\\,k\\in [-0.5,0.5]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a horizontal line segment from the point $(-0.5,0.5)$ to the point $(0.5,0.5)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/509d3e8d3bce0b422cad7e7f02377468cacc0de3a4e45647c12fef15f2c9991f.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[-i,i]\\times \\{1-i\\}=\\{(k,1-i)\\,:\\,k\\in [-i,i]\\}\n$$\n\nis the horizontal line segment from the point $(-i,1-i)$ to the point $(i,1-i).$ \n\n![](images/21/21859c2ac02ba80d47672bf2fab25f7358feb921bc5b546c1e8c783c2819bea3.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following triangle that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}:$\n\n![](images/5b/5b5715ba2a1b71a9f5aef52882ed62ba2fe1610c143224914430f79fdc8f06b5.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287874 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287874 #edb/ref 1069 "q-184506" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287874 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287874 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $I=[0,2]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=\\{i\\}\\times [-i,i].$ Which of the following represents ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287874 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1c878a8c-80cf-5939-80cc-1ff04a9bef2d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287874 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=1,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}=\\{1\\}\\times [-1,1]=\\{(1,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [-1,1]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(1,-1)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/f2/f26fa8a525806370f5505ad097ead2b289192bb3a6eb3e341eb7c2f91a03531f.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{i\\}\\times [-i,i]=\\{(i,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [-i,i]\\}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,-i)$ to the point $(i,i).$ \n\n![](images/bf/bfeafe4c3b800b33b4f8550de02c2eb908effb5c28ef59b40f8c5e2bcad38ac4.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following triangle that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}:$\n\n![](images/4d/4d860b60ab9e139b26c9f6e1d330188d5e752a51624a2374ad713b6974e66608.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287877 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287877 #edb/ref 1069 "q-184530" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287877 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287877 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=[0,1]\\times [0,i].$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287877 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e59a2427-468e-5dd7-9dc4-516ebb0d95d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287877 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[0,1]\\times [0,0.5]=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [0,1],b\\in [0,0.5]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a rectangle whose vertices are at $(0,0),$ $(0,1),$ $(0.5,1),$ and $(0.5,0)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/0f/0fef44c4b8c4911a892d7dad4502edbb80ef236553d6fddb57de1472bd44a62b.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[0,1]\\times [0,i]=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [0,1],b\\in [0,i]\\}\n$$\n\nis the rectangle whose vertices are at $(0,0),$ $(0,1),$ $(i,1),$ and $(i,0).$\n\n![](images/f4/f4602a46d3e4fc1799a475f05548ef278fdd33d354d1ec19ca4e5c5cc34cfcf8.png)\n\nThe intersection of all these rectangles is the line segments $[0,1]\\times \\{0\\}:$\n\n![](images/19/19a2cd951a55508269a0034aec582345bf7e350ec516fe1eecac0d6bf5847981.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389798 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389798 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389798 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389798 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389798 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389798 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389798 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389798 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186389800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389798 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f376ba00-5dab-590e-a791-4441f99a66cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389799 #edb/ref 1080 "images/c8/c87871c50b343792c73fc18dfe92bc2065b18ed3de1e7bb70324b8b43d027206.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389799 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389799 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c00cf5df-4240-5b04-850f-1b4b0d6f8ae1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389800 #edb/ref 1080 "images/39/39db7bc1b259b222a2365441babc3421101961ea26a7143af14b526ba7f81200.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389800 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389800 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1f633a02-8088-56db-88c2-7889ba264321" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389801 #edb/ref 1080 "images/ec/ec0e1df36bf5d93b8cdbcc3d14cd510fbe299b929ed3d19f4d901f4461093305.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389801 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389801 #edb/